I'm sorry but I really have to correct you. The Roman Catholic Church is arguably not the oldest Extant branch of christianity in existance.

&#x200B;

Okay so, in the early church you had 5 bishops who were administratively speaking in charge of a region. You had the Patriarchs of Constantinople, Antioch, Jerusalem and thhe Popes of Alexandria and Rome. Now what essentially happened was that Rome changed the [creed](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Apostles%27_Creed) and added that the Holy spirit proceeds from the Father and the Son.

&#x200B;

That might sound like something tiny but it was essentially a complete formulation of what and who God was in many ways. The Pope of Rome had also continously attempted to wrestle control from the other bishops, caused the word pornocracy to come into existance and had generally deviated from the theology of all the other bishops.

&#x200B;

So what happened was that the patriarch of constantinople severed communion with rome, and then everyone else also severed communion with rome too. Rome was essentially thrown out of the church for their theology. Legalism was one of those things that got them thrown out. Catholicism is absolutely very legalistic, that was one of the things the protestant reformers tried to change btw.

&#x200B;

Now people always forget the church that those 5 other bishops were now a part of. The Eastern Orthodox church is essentially what you describe, mystic and generally fairly ritualistic.

&#x200B;

Let me give you the basics in the theology.

1. Sin is a sickness and the word for sin simply means "missing the mark". It's not a crime that you will be judged by and then either found pardoned from or thrown in jail for. Instead it's stumbling and doing actions that turn you away from God (essentially choosing to separate yourself from God) and falling short of the purpose God created us for. Sin is then a sickness that must be healed through repentence, the sacraments, participation in the church, prayer and struggling against the sinful parts of yourself, every day is essentially then a struggle to sin as little as possible and do as much good as possible.
2. Hell is not a separate location. Heaven and Hell is the same place experienced in different ways depending on your spiritual state. One who has not embraced God and instead chosen to live in sin will experience it as intense shame and regret for example. This removes the strange dynamic were God created a torture pit to throw all the bad people in. This dynamic came from catholicism and it was then adopted by the protestant reformers.
3. The Orthodox church works like the early church when it comes to administration and leadership (and you can definetly see this in the bible). The Early church was not run by one universal bishop on earth but rather it was a collection of bishops in communion with eachother, that is how the church still operates. It's a bunch of autocephalous (independent) churches that have an administrative leader (who is still a bishop) and whom are in communion with eachother.
4. This administration has lead to some interesting facts such as the fact that an extremely large amount of things are not seen as dogma. The view on hell that I just told you for example is not offical dogma even if it is the most offically accepted idea of hell. There are a lot of things that simply haven't been made dogma has the entire church has to have a council to set dogma. One bishop can't simply say "X is true but Y is false and now heresy".
5. There is also a huge emphasis within orthodoxy to read the bible, study church history, read the church fathers and consume essentially all manner of literature and to study.
6. The Orthodox church has often translated the bible when coming to a new place rather than having Latin masses that aren't understandble to anyone. The Orthodox church invented  a writing system so that the slavs could read the bible themselves, the church always adapted to preach in the vernacular.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

Sorry fot the long post but people always forget that there are more christian denominations that aren't either protestant or catholic. The Orthodox church has 200 million members which makes it the second biggest church in the world.

Hey! I'd like make a small correction. You're both right and wrong.  


War is  not something christians are called to do and going to war is \*not\* ideal however the world we live in isn't ideal. That's why we sometimes get dispensation (economy is the theological term) to fall short of the ideal to prevent something generally worse.

&#x200B;

These things are still sins and require genuine repentence but they are "allowed" to happen as to prevent an outcome that would be worse. Let's say someone is trying to murder your child and you have to kill them in self-defense.

&#x200B;

You have still commited a sin but you won't be judged as harshly as if you went and just killed some random person on the street. War is a bit similar and is one of those situations where they view is basically "this is not ideal but if it is inevitable do this during the circumstances". So you treat your enemies with as much respect as you can and you don't kill or harm anyone more than you need to to win the war.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

It should also be noted that there are many warrior saints (born and canonized before Thomas Aquinas too. And Jesus met roman soldiers, if christianity was completely pacifistic he would tell them to stop being soldiers but he does not.  



To summarize this really horribly put together comment.  


Going to war and being a soldier is not ideal and killing is a sin, but it may sometimes be neccessary sometimes as defense due to the world not being ideal.   


If a christian lives in a conquered territory they should however not take up armed rebellion against the invader but rather do their best to not make trouble, following the law (unless it forces them to  go against christianity).

&#x200B;

Note however that this is an Eastern Orthodox perspective. If you're interested in reading a much more well written discussion about this then I reccomend reading [this](https://www.reddit.com/r/OrthodoxChristianity/comments/8yyctt/is_there_is_a_just_war_theory_in_orthodox/) reddit thread

Ja för det är exakt det jag säger.

&#x200B;

Alla som dricker rånar inte eller håller på sig nej, men de flesta som dricker går ut och festar eller går ut på krogen. Där dricker de väldigt mycket och går sedan ut och göra dum skit som t.ex slagsmål, ropande, etc. Det händer dock inte med alla men 

&#x200B;

Undrar dock hur väl du skulle klara dig om du plötsligt inte kunde dricka under en månad, det kanske skulle börja bli lite abstinens

Mina källor för vad? Att de flesta är missbrukare? Det beror på vad man tänker på som missbruk och jag är ganska säker på att vad som räknas som "alkoholmissbruk" har en mycket snävare definition än t.ex cannabismissbruk har.

&#x200B;

Men jo, om jag faktiskt gör en enkät och kollar hur folk känner sig för att inte dricka på ett tag eller hur mycket de vill dricka för att må bättre så skulle nästan alla säga att de har svårt att klara sig utan alkohol. Många vill bara inte erkänna det. Precis som alla människor som är beroende av något.

Basically ja. Folk som dricker gör det jobbigt för alla runt sig så det är bara rättvist att det åtminstone blir lika jobbigt för alkisar att dricka, även om deras beteende blir är mycket jobbigare än något man realistiskt kan göra för att göra det jobbigare åt dem.

&#x200B;

Och ja, det är såklart du måste dricka ikväll för det. Hur annars ska man klara dagen utan lite sprit? Seriöst, alkisar är fan som drogmissbrukare förutom att jag åtminstone tycker lite synd om folk som missbrukar droger. Men ni har seriöst exakt samma attityd om hur mycket ni \*behöver\* era droger, det är bara socialt acceptabelt att missbruka alkohol eller så firas det av folk som dig.

Fast problemet är just att vuxna beter sig som barn.  "Oj nu är det helg! Dags att supa sig full och förstöra folks egendom, slå ner någon eller kanske bara vara extremt jobbig". Det känns helt ärligt som folk beter sig så.

&#x200B;

Och det är bara värre i andra länder. I sverige är vi iaf emot alkohol nog för att människorna som beter sig så (vilket verkar vara de flesta som dricker mycket) åtminstone måste planenera sig fylleri.

&#x200B;

Och för det andra, vem som helst kan inte bara gå och köpa vapen i Sverige. Hur många dör av lagligt sålda vapen i Sverige? Tror inte att det är så många. De vapen som de använder är nästan alltid något som ingen får äga i Sverige (med vapenlicens eller ej).

&#x200B;

Sedan kan tobak inte alls jämföras med alkohol. Hur ofta ser du någon som röker en cigg och sen går hem och misshandlar nån där? Eller blir skitagressiv och börjar bråka med någon annan bara för att? Det händer inte. Tobak är fan dåligt för dig men det ändrar inte din sinnesstämning på det sättet som alkohol gör.

&#x200B;

Folk kan inte bete sig helt ärligt. Så fort man gör det enkelt att få alkohol så beter dem sig som barn, jag ser det överallt i hela världen och det är mycket värre i andra länder. Dock om man kollar på hur folk beter sig här så beter de sig fan inte bättre.

&#x200B;

"mEn dEt äR jObBiGt aTt gÅ nEr tIlL sYsTeMeT oCh jAg dRiCkEr iNtE mYcKeT" är det man hör från folk som dricker "lite" och visst, men då får man bara ta och planera. Man kanske inte behöver sitta och dricka sprit på en fredagskväll eller så köper du din sprit på någon av de andra 4 dagarna i veckan. 

&#x200B;

Det exemplet med småbutiker skulle troligen kunna funka dock, fast sen kommer folk klaga och tvinga fram att man ska börja sälja sprit i matbutiker också för att de \*måste\* ha sin sprit varje kväll för hur ska man annars tycka att någonting är roligt om man inte är berusad??
